  "reason": "The records collectively point in the right direction: R6 establishes the exact AlCoCrFeNi2.1 FCC/B2 eutectic system; R2 highlights the importance of FCC/B2 interface morphology and coherency in a close Al-Co-Cr-Fe-Ni alloy; R3, R8, R10, and R17 show that intermediate-temperature aging can tune ordered phases and strongly alter strength and ductility; and R4, R13, R14, and R19 connect chemical ordering, dislocation locking or restricted slip, temperature-dependent fault energetics, and plastic instability. In particular, R17 supplies a closely related high-entropy Ni-Al-containing FCC/L12 architecture. However, none of the records identifies the specific 600°C maximum in L12 order, the nearly Ni3Al-stoichiometric ordered FCC domains, jagged B2/FCC boundary tips, or their coupled role in producing a ductile-brittle-ductile sequence followed by recovery near 750°C. An expert could reasonably propose aging the exact eutectic alloy and examining ordering and interfaces, but the specific two-temperature intervention and coupled mechanism would still require a substantial new idea."
